sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, ADR 0241 allowlists, DECISIONS R1-R4 (rules, admission); ADR 0271 and docs/reference/gateway-engine.md (execution). Same shape as firewall.allow.
  %% Engine flow: the kind runs on the Gateway state machine when ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS lists it and the target Node's executor reported `ufw.ensure_rule`; otherwise it still runs the Swamp workflow operation-firewall-direct, which goes once every Node reports.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: firewall:remove
  %% kind: firewall.remove
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=executes tone=blue
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>state machine
  participant N as Target Node<br/>agent executor
  CLI->>GW: firewall:remove PORT<br/>--node=NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:remove PORT --node=NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the destination port or port range of the rule, and the Node
  %%> - optionally the source and the protocol, to pick one rule when several share the port
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - the CLI asks before removal; `--yes` confirms without a prompt
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node
  %%> - the target is an active Linux Node
  %%> - exactly one rule on the target matches the port, source and protocol: none is `firewall.rule_not_found`, several is `firewall.rule_ambiguous` and lists each match
  %%> - no other firewall change runs on the target; the Gateway holds one lock per Node
  %%> Works out what to keep:
  %%> - a ufw line that another rule, or a rule Orbit itself needs, also uses stays on the Node (`retain`)
  %%> - only lines that belong to this rule alone are removed
  Note over GW: Record step, start event<br/>ensure-0 · orbit.engine.start
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the rule is recorded as removing, with the full intent and the engine step `ensure-0`
  %%> - the start event goes to JetStream; the state machine takes it from there
  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the Operation ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the Operation with `operation:show`
  GW->>N: Send the signed order<br/>ufw.ensure_rule · NATS
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - signs one order for the target Node: operation `<id>:ensure-0`, target the Node itself, so its firewall changes run one at a time
  %%> - publishes it to the Node's durable consumer on the ORDERS stream, over NATS on WireGuard
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - `action`, `port`, `protocol`, `source`, `comment`, `ensure: absent`, `retain`
  %%> Deadline:
  %%> - a JetStream schedule wakes the step after 10 s; with no receipt by then, a new order goes out; after 120 s the step fails with `engine.step_budget_exceeded`
  %%> - an order older than its 8 s expiry is refused as `expired`, and the Gateway sends a new one
  Note over N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status numbered
  %%> How:
  %%> - the executor checks the order's signature, then reads ufw's numbered rows
  %%> - refuses an inactive firewall (`firewall.backend_inactive`) before any change
  Note over N: Plan the changes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - turns the rule into one ufw delete command per protocol and address family
  %%> - skips a line that is already gone and a line that must stay
  %%> - plans no changes when no line is left to delete
  %%> How:
  %%> - the typed primitive `ufw.ensure_rule` in the executor (crate `firewall`, a port of the former Swamp planner)
  alt changes needed
    Note over N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv per change
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - deletes each line that belongs to this rule alone; a line that is already gone is skipped
    %%> - a line that a role also needs (`retain`) stays
    %%> - runs the planned commands one at a time
    Note over N: Read the result<br/>ufw status numbered
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the rule only counts as done when ufw shows it after the changes
  else already removed
    Note over N: Skipped — already removed<br/>firewall.rule_absent
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the plan contains no changes
    %%> - there is no second read of ufw: the check runs on the first read, which nothing has changed since
  end
  Note over N: Check the rules
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks every removed line is gone, and retained lines are still there
  %%> - after changes, a line that ufw changed in between fails the check, and a rerun repairs it
  alt end state is right
    N-->>GW: Receipt: applied<br/>changes N
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - the receipt goes to the RECEIPTS stream; the order is acknowledged only after JetStream stored it
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule record is deleted from the Gateway and the Operation succeeds, through the kind's projection
    %%> - `changes: 0` reports `Skipped — already removed (firewall.rule_absent)`, part of the successful result, not an error
  else removing or checking fails
    N--xGW: Receipt: failed<br/>error_code
    %%> When:
    %%> - ufw refuses the change, or the end state is wrong (`firewall.verify_failed`)
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule stays, marked failed with `failed_step: ensure-0`, so you can see it
    %%> - the Operation fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - lines removed before the failure stay removed; there is no rollback
    %%> - that is safe: the rule stays marked failed, and a rerun skips lines that are already gone
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new Operation
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the Operation's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the Operation has ended
  end
